{
  "image_name": "0c1283188bd4aed0a4fd06d68814581f.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how loss of chromosome Y (LOY) may reshape immune-cell communication by altering receptor–ligand pairs between different cell types. Antigen-presenting cells (APCs) are shown at the center interacting with CD4+ and CD8+ T cells, regulatory T cells, NK cells, and microglia, while a second scheme shows monocyte–dendritic cell interactions. Colored labels indicate whether molecules are increased or decreased in LOY cells and whether the resulting interaction is thought to be inhibitory, activating, mixed, or unclear.\n\nOverall, the strongest visible pattern is that LOY tends to favor inhibitory checkpoint signaling in several T-cell interactions. Examples include increased TIGIT, PD1/PDL1, TIM3, and LAG3-related pathways, suggesting dampened CD4+, CD8+, and Treg cell responses. In contrast, NK cells are marked as having an activating effect of LOY, with decreases in inhibitory receptors such as TIGIT and LAG3. Microglial effects appear uncertain, with reduced CD226 noted.\n\nThe monocyte–dendritic cell panel suggests a more mixed, subtype-specific effect. Several monocyte surface molecules are reduced, including CD86, TIGIT, CD226, CD96, and CD47, while CD112 and TIM3 are increased. Together, the diagram conveys that LOY may broadly rewire immune regulation by shifting checkpoint and adhesion signaling in a cell-type-dependent way.",
